#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/494cd45f9c85aaa8703ed4c5a0cac805b69cd91bbef979a2c3ff1ffac1592763.png)" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/4db4bdfa8aaeac26238404107f195847eaccde9e595a7d1a0678053e2b149d0e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161751 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\n- The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14050120/assets/f8d153708f7872d0c96414a77c12379d40c6b60a5f08844945fb8a8f8c1809ab.png)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eedf2233-cd35-51dd-ba8a-acdfb24fada6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328760} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dbaa75-a5a2-5947-9bbd-cbd505eedb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328761} {:answer/id #uuid "1abd424b-ab9d-567a-aa9a-392f2bf8a6ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328762} {:answer/id #uuid "e02c8b56-9c90-5ce6-98c1-e9cb055d4d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328763} {:answer/id #uuid "a11ef24c-e275-5da0-b9cc-7a5868387040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1abd424b-ab9d-567a-aa9a-392f2bf8a6ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328762} :answer-field/id #uuid "5528bf15-c00b-5418-b305-184e01d61a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d998fa5b-63b3-55ca-a8aa-c2f7bc2f2a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140504" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c076c3e4-ca30-53e1-9053-0585f557ff4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328767} {:answer/id #uuid "43ebfd72-668a-55f2-ae3e-c80c0a87e537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328768} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee6595b-4a0e-553c-8249-72f4b6b609b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328769} {:answer/id #uuid "13755144-f63e-5350-80a1-c1a769e70153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328770} {:answer/id #uuid "f1eaa499-034f-5dec-b795-eabdd59655b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c076c3e4-ca30-53e1-9053-0585f557ff4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a310c5ec-c97a-55cb-ad91-cc33ccba7dea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14c9b2e9-b22a-597e-94a1-26642435278d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143679" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "766e4edc-9c20-52d2-ba07-30b45cf17548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328774} {:answer/id #uuid "a0809ce5-7f55-5a1a-96e8-10a9a1319ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328775} {:answer/id #uuid "542bd1b8-1583-5548-b823-b7431bbe37b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328776} {:answer/id #uuid "211c1391-358f-542c-9cdf-06e7dfdb3005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328777} {:answer/id #uuid "3d86f638-dca4-55cb-8596-660f927e91e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "211c1391-358f-542c-9cdf-06e7dfdb3005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328777} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7f3d44-6845-5a05-847d-9e6bf6d0e9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fade6e6-11ef-58fb-9f20-801a35d8d789" :question/math-academy-id "q-143685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7b53d54-f0c0-5965-afc7-73ae56fbbcbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328781} {:answer/id #uuid "aa31387c-cd85-51b9-8862-c57c0471b878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328782} {:answer/id #uuid "12d875b7-1eb9-5a1f-86fc-65485512c323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328783} {:answer/id #uuid "f75489ec-43cf-568d-b608-c83e16d5847a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328784} {:answer/id #uuid "608d273b-3192-5dc3-860a-a370ce327f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa31387c-cd85-51b9-8862-c57c0471b878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328782} :answer-field/id #uuid "452114cb-45c6-54be-98a0-4f549aec1dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ea3443f-216b-53ab-8156-54dba0b0f421" :question/math-academy-id "q-143686" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be50ba1-87ed-5e49-bb3d-1a6da1c3686d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160705} {:answer/id #uuid "d45127db-5388-5643-94dd-467ae055143c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160706} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2f65fb-64fb-5493-b427-86dee9cec2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160707} {:answer/id #uuid "431c09e0-a899-57af-bee3-4c677834790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160708} {:answer/id #uuid "54ec641a-3d94-5b8a-a8f1-4a8b01f21fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "431c09e0-a899-57af-bee3-4c677834790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160708} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ac9f5a-177e-5127-b3dc-d2e82a45d0f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd69c0a4-182b-55d3-9077-6d410397ebbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-93583" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d2c524d-75c8-509f-8e3a-4dc728edd52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328788} {:answer/id #uuid "fa984d91-9c17-5957-ab68-40dbb92a0d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328789} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8667dd-b3a9-52c2-8135-496dca8c2f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328790} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9aecc-eb6d-5498-9bbe-69d0e051fe7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328791} {:answer/id #uuid "958014bb-2921-5227-a9a3-13c2c2c9d303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8667dd-b3a9-52c2-8135-496dca8c2f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328790} :answer-field/id #uuid "559ed0ee-30e4-55d6-a67b-7f25a3481dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3b547ce-eddc-5153-a8ef-15edfde23fe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "267f83ee-a054-5845-b9de-d36cda768259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328795} {:answer/id #uuid "a83873b8-2132-54f4-8d14-e585901b42d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328796} {:answer/id #uuid "5824191e-ed48-5107-b882-fd6c45280102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328797} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4003ac-da2d-5dc2-8be8-2c6e8deeef4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328798} {:answer/id #uuid "d930335b-0e39-59bc-a451-12eb58f6206e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5824191e-ed48-5107-b882-fd6c45280102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328797} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6859063-5785-5bc0-af31-6858d48a5f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2af9cef0-49a6-59d3-80eb-25677266088b" :question/math-academy-id "q-93993" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9507583-82df-5e08-bb5f-660d9b69bd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161281} {:answer/id #uuid "dc91df55-e685-5117-8e85-b79be9615e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161282} {:answer/id #uuid "e4821da8-58a7-52f5-80d2-25706c1e50a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161283} {:answer/id #uuid "f949c9bd-138a-5696-bfaa-40fbc83229c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161284} {:answer/id #uuid "73e81aa7-98d4-59a5-828f-3ab837cc556d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9507583-82df-5e08-bb5f-660d9b69bd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161281} :answer-field/id #uuid "d849989a-e4ca-50ea-9440-10e999440302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be226ee-498f-567c-82d1-206ed205336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96046" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457390a0-5131-5259-acb4-ecfeccf19c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328802} {:answer/id #uuid "493aa98f-b668-58dd-931c-ca7dc32fa045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328803} {:answer/id #uuid "98227d7d-cca3-5c23-a956-ad1eec43a74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328804} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5860e9-2de7-5b1f-9199-643c31716303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328805} {:answer/id #uuid "8adbf214-32e8-55c6-a088-88fb93e6f038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "457390a0-5131-5259-acb4-ecfeccf19c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328802} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7455d6-80b1-5c51-8e56-df2b42a9e44d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ce569a7-84a0-586c-9965-dabc3caf2d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-96592" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c93db6-10c6-54cf-bcd1-b57b5bad1d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328809} {:answer/id #uuid "85f41446-ca33-54f1-911a-a408b069a8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328810} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc01e52-6557-5e4e-9ef7-b2fb521d41d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811} {:answer/id #uuid "14505df5-25cb-51ff-99da-06d39c311c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328812} {:answer/id #uuid "a13dbc57-e2d1-59a3-916f-ab9772b20162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bc01e52-6557-5e4e-9ef7-b2fb521d41d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811} :answer-field/id #uuid "a841c9e2-3bab-55ca-8b19-66c8b02779fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e570eafc-b929-5b7a-8999-21d9d7a319e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96596" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is symmetric. And, since the sum to two symmetric matrices is symmetric, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
